After the interview
After the interview, the candidate should be discussed by those who interviewed them. The objective isn’t necessarily to get to consensus first (though that too, ultimately), but rather to get to questions and areas of concern. In this regard, the post-interview conversation must be handled carefully: the interview is deliberately constructed to allow broad contact with the candidate, and it is possible that some will see something that others may miss.

To get to those concerns, the post-interview conversation should include each person who interviewed a candidate presenting their impressions (by convention, in the same order as the interview schedule). In some (and perhaps many!) cases, interviewers will be unequivocal that they would like to work with the candidate — but it is also common for interviews to result in open questions or explicit concerns. Even if or where there is overwhelming positivity about a candidate, questions and concerns should be treated seriously. In some cases, a single person seeing something can be disqualifying (especially if it indicates a values mismatch or — worse — a breach of Oxide’s principles), but in most others it will pose a collective question: did others have the same question or concern? In this regard, it’s important that interviewers listen to the experiences of one another: sometimes someone has a question or a concern that others didn’t voice but also share; sometimes the question or concern had by one has been explicitly and satisfactorily addressed in another conversation.

If the questions or concerns have been adequately addressed (and assuming conversations were otherwise positive), the discussion can be brief, positive and forward-looking. If concerns or questions remain, however, the discussion should focus on addressing them. If the collective conversations cannot address them (and if they can’t be answered by the corpus of material provided by the candidate), the group should together brainstorm about how to resolve it. Presuming that the questions or concerns are not immediately disqualifying, the objective should be to construct a small number of follow-up conversations that are focused on the issue of concern. In this regard, it can be very helpful to have people present for the discussion that weren’t on the original interview schedule: hiring discussions are not recorded, and having heard everyone’s perspective on a particular issue equips another employee particularly well for a focused follow-up conversation. We have found that it generally doesn’t take more than a follow-up conversation or two to resolve the concerns one way or the other, but there is no set process here: follow-up conversations should continue to be held as necessary to gain consensus and conviction. (And if it needs to be said: if multiple follow-up conversations are not resolving the concerns, that itself is an indicator that the concerns are real enough to merit not moving forward.)